    "## Data"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Instructions: REPLACE the contents of this cell with your work\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Explain what the **ideal** dataset you would want to answer this question. (This should include: \n",
    "   1. What variables? \n",
    "   2. How many observations are needed? \n",
    "   3. Who/what/how would these data be collected? \n",
    "   4. How would these data be stored/organized?\n",
    "\n",
    "To answer the research question “How well can we predict year-normalized sales scores (‘Bestseller Level’) of a video game based only on its pre-release characteristics?”, the ideal dataset would consist of game-level observations, where each row corresponds to a single video game release.\n",
    "\n",
    "A. \n",
    "The ideal dataset would include two types of variables: \n",
    "Pre-release characteristics (predictors): \n",
    "Release year\n",
    "Platform (e.g., PS4, Xbox, PC)\n",
    "Genre (e.g., Action, RPG, Shooter)\n",
    "Publisher\n",
    "Developer (optional, if available)\n",
    "These variables are all observable before a game is released, ensuring that the prediction task reflects a realistic ex-ante decision-making scenario.\n",
    "Outcome variable:Year-normalized sales score (“Bestseller Level”), defined as a game’s percentile rank of global sales among all games released in the same year.\n",
    "This outcome captures the degree of commercial success while controlling for changes in overall market size across time.\n",
    "\n",
    "B. \n",
    "How many observations are needed?\n",
    "To support reliable predictive modeling with multiple categorical variables, the dataset should contain several thousand observations. A large sample size is especially important due to the high dimensionality introduced by platforms and publishers, and to ensure sufficient variation across years and genres.\n",
    "\n",
    "C. \n",
    "Who/what/how would these data be collected?\n",
    "Ideally, the data would be collected retrospectively from:\n",
    "Industry databases tracking video game releases and global sales\n",
    "Aggregated reports from publishers and platform holders\n",
    "Release metadata would be compiled from public records, while sales figures would be aggregated at the game level to construct the year-normalized sales score.\n",
    "\n",
    "D. \n",
    "How would these data be stored/organized?\n",
    "The dataset would be stored in a tabular format (e.g., CSV), where:\n",
    "Each row represents a single video game\n",
    "Each column represents a variable (release year, platform, genre, publisher, etc.)\n",
    "Categorical variables would be stored as labeled fields and encoded during the analysis stage.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Search for potential **real** datasets that could provide you with something useful for this project.  For each dataset that you find write 3-5 sentences describing \n",
    "   1. where the data is located (e.g., URL) and anything you need to do to use it (e.g., ask for permission, fill out an application)\n",
    "   2. what the important variables are in this dataset that you might use\n",
    "\n",
    "Dataset 1: \n",
    "Video Game Sales Dataset (Kaggle)\n",
    "Location: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/anandshaw2001/video-game-sales\n",
    "Access: Publicly available; no special permission required\n",
    "Description:\n",
    "This dataset contains sales data for over 16,000 video games, including release year, platform, genre, publisher, and regional and global sales. Although sales are observed post-release, they can be used to construct a year-normalized sales score, while only pre-release variables are used as predictors.\n",
    "Relevant variables:\n",
    "Release year, platform, genre, publisher, global sales\n",
    "\n",
    "Dataset 2: \n",
    "RAWG Video Games Dataset\n",
    "Location: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/atalaydenknalbant/rawg-games-dataset\n",
    "Access: Public dataset subject to RAWG’s data usage terms\n",
    "Description:\n",
    "This dataset provides detailed pre-release metadata on video games collected independently from the sales dataset. Unlike Dataset 1, which primarily contains high-level categorical information and post-release sales outcomes, the RAWG dataset offers richer and more granular descriptors of games prior to release, including detailed genre tags, developer information, and precise release dates. While it does not include sales figures, this dataset is necessary to expand the space of pre-release characteristics and to assess whether additional metadata improves the predictability of a game’s year-normalized sales score. Using a second, independently sourced dataset also reduces reliance on a single data-generating process and strengthens the robustness of the predictive analysis.\n",
    "Relevant variables:\n",
    "Release date, platform, genre tags, publisher, developer\n",
    "\n",
    "  "
   ]
